Fix segmentation fault on a SAM header carrying no sort-order field.

checkHeader() stripped the trailing newline from the @HD SO: value BEFORE
testing whether that value had been found at all, so an @HD line with no SO:
tag left `order` NULL and the trim dereferenced it. The guard below restores
the intended order of operations: trim only when a sort order was actually
present. A missing sort order is then handled by the check immediately after,
which already tests for NULL and raises ERRSORT when -S is in effect.

Backported verbatim from upstream commit
da439890cfcbf8feaff473e8e703dceae2d385c0 ("debug error from SAM w/o
sort-order", 2023-05-08). That commit sits on jsh58/Genrich master AFTER the
v0.6.1 tag this recipe builds from, and upstream has published no release
since v0.6.1, so the fix reaches no tagged tarball. Rebased onto v0.6.1: the
hunk applies with no fuzz at an offset of -21 lines, the surrounding context
being byte-identical in the two revisions.

Only the bug fix is taken. The remaining post-v0.6.1 commits on upstream
master implement the -L genome-length option, which is a new feature and is
deliberately NOT carried here.
diff --git a/Genrich.c b/Genrich.c
index 8bd6d59..e36e9f3 100644
--- a/Genrich.c
+++ b/Genrich.c
@@ -4301,10 +4301,12 @@ void checkHeader(char* line, int* chromLen, Chrom** chrom,
         order = field + 3;
       field = strtok(NULL, TAB);
     }
-    // removing trailing '\n'
-    int i;
-    for (i = 0; order[i] != '\n' && order[i] != '\0'; i++) ;
-    order[i] = '\0';
+    if (order != NULL) {
+      // removing trailing '\n'
+      int i;
+      for (i = 0; order[i] != '\n' && order[i] != '\0'; i++) ;
+      order[i] = '\0';
+    }
 
     // sort order must be queryname
     if (sortOpt && (order == NULL
